{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's answer hinges on the 'correction event' as the single-evaluation evidence distinguishing genuine reliability gain from mere compliance. But the evidence it actually specifies for that distinction is backwards: it classifies the panel as 'compliance luck' when internal notes show the panel ignored the checklist items that would have led to the correct answer. A panel that reaches the correct answer without using the checklist items that would have led there is by definition not complying with the checklist in the relevant sense; it is exhibiting exactly the same unforced-correctness condition that the target's own overturn condition admits provides no checklist reliability gain. Conversely, the target never identifies the case where the panel ticks every checklist box and reaches the right answer purely because the box happens to match the outcome — i.e. genuine checklist compliance that merely tracks the right answer. That is the central comparator the problem asks the evidence to separate, and SRC_001's two buckets ('correction event' vs 'ignored checklist') do not partition it. An observation of full compliance plus a correct answer could be a genuine gain or could be the mere tracking the problem warns about, and nothing in the mechanism distinguishes them.","counterexample":[{"panel_a":"ticks every checklist box, internal notes show each item considered and matched, correct answer produced — indistinguishable from SRC_001's genuine gain, yet could be mere checklist compliance tracking the answer","panel_b":"ticks every checklist box, no correction event because initial intuition was already correct, correct answer produced — fits 'no reliability gain' but is not flagged by the 'compliance luck' branch, which only fires when notes show ignored items"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 proposes that reliability 'reduces to' a choice between omission and misinterpretation, but its own refutation condition and its stated integrative relation both import exactly the factor that reduction would eliminate. If reliability is genuinely reduced to an omission/misinterpretation choice, then a task whose failures are systemic (i.e. systemic misinterpretation) should be cleanly handled by the expert-intuition branch; there should be no residual role for high interdependency or systemic-versus-ommissive structure. Yet the refuting condition is the existence of a high-interdependency task that a checklist-led panel solves more reliably than an expert when the failure mode is systemic. The mere coherence of that refuting case shows that the target content is really claiming 'reliability reduces to error-type distribution, except when task structure overrides it,' which is not a reduction to a two-mode choice. The content thus offers a substantive integration in the same breath as a reduction and lets the two relations compete without resolving which one is being claimed.","counterexample":[{"task":"high-interdependency, systemic-failure-mode task where the checklist-led panel is more reliable than the expert — the stated refuting condition — which should be impossible if reliability strictly reduces to omission-vs-misinterpretation, since systemic failure is already in the misinterpretation branch assigned to expert intuition"}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_003 is coherent as a substantive relation: it names a contradiction between SRC_003's intuition advantage for high-interdependency tasks and SRC_002's premise, gives the precise condition under which the expert gestalt advantage is diminished (when trap variables in SRC_002 are themselves interdependent and the checklist neutralizes them), and supplies a genuine refuting condition (checklists proven incapable of guiding non-experts through non-linear interactions). The problem's only requirement is to name a kind of relation and state what it is refuted if; the target does both. An objection could note that the trap-variable mechanism is stipulated rather than independently warranted, but the relation is still substantive and the content never claims proof of that mechanism — it rests the relation's truth on it. Under the stated standard, that is a fault-free claim-and-refutation structure.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_003"}]}